## Why it breaks

The CSS `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` and GSAP's `x`/`y` both write to the same `transform` property on the same node. When the tween starts, GSAP parses the existing transform into pixel values and then overwrites it. That causes two problems:

- **Lost centering:** the percentage offset either drifts or snaps away.
- **Lint error:** `gsap_css_transform_conflict` fails `lint`. A lint error also disables the layout and contrast audits in `check`, so they report `0 sample(s)`.

The fix is to keep exactly one owner of `transform`.

## Recommended fix: center with layout, animate with GSAP

A full-bleed flex wrapper does the centering, so the heading carries no CSS transform. GSAP then owns `transform` completely.

```html
<div id="title-stage" class="title-stage">
  <h1 id="title-heading" class="title">Your Heading</h1>
</div>

<style>
  .title-stage {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
  }
  .title {
    display: block;   /* transformed elements must be block-level + sized */
    margin: 0;
    /* no transform here */
  }
</style>

<script>
  const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
  tl.fromTo("#title-heading",
    { y: 40, opacity: 0 },
    { y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power2.out" },
    0.2
  );
  window.__timelines["<root data-composition-id>"] = tl;
</script>
```

## Alternative: keep absolute centering, but let GSAP own the offset

If the heading has to stay `position: absolute` at `top/left: 50%`, remove the CSS `translate` and express the offset with `xPercent`/`yPercent`. GSAP composes those with `x`/`y`, so centering and motion coexist.

```css
.title { position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%; margin: 0; /* no transform */ }
```

```js
tl.fromTo("#title-heading",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 40, opacity: 0 },
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power2.out" },
  0.2
);
```

Put `xPercent`/`yPercent` in both the from and the to values. That way the heading is centered at every seek position, including before the tween starts.

## Determinism checklist for this element

- **One paused timeline per composition:** register it at `window.__timelines["<id>"]`, where the key equals the root `data-composition-id`. Register only after the timeline is fully built.
- **Explicit start values:** use `fromTo`, not `from`, so seeking to any frame gives the same result.
- **Fixed inputs only:** use no clocks, unseeded `Math.random`, or measurement-dependent values in the tween.
- **No `autoAlpha` or `visibility` on a `.clip`:** if the heading or its wrapper is a `.clip`, animate the inner `<h1>`, as shown above.
- **No scene-exit `visibility: hidden` set:** the runtime already hides timed clips.

