## Why it breaks

GSAP keeps its own transform state and writes the whole `transform` property on every tween frame. A CSS `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)` on the same node gets overwritten when the `x`/`y` tween starts, so the heading jumps off-center. `lint` flags this as `gsap_css_transform_conflict`.

## Fix A (preferred): center with layout, animate only offsets

Use flex or `inset` for centering, so `transform` belongs only to GSAP.

```html
<div class="heading-wrap">
  <h1 id="hero-heading" class="heading">Title</h1>
</div>
```

```css
.heading-wrap {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
.heading {
  display: block;          /* transformed elements must be block-level */
  margin: 0;
  /* no transform here */
}
```

```js
const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
tl.fromTo("#hero-heading",
  { x: -40, y: 20, opacity: 0 },
  { x: 0, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power3.out" },
  0
);
window.__timelines["main"] = tl; // key must match root data-composition-id
```

## Fix B: keep absolute 50/50 positioning, move the percent offset into GSAP

```css
.heading {
  position: absolute;
  left: 50%;
  top: 50%;
  display: block;
  margin: 0;
  /* no transform here */
}
```

```js
tl.fromTo("#hero-heading",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, x: -40, y: 20, opacity: 0 },
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, x: 0,   y: 0,  opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power3.out" },
  0
);
```

GSAP composes `xPercent`/`yPercent` with `x`/`y`, so the heading stays centered while the pixel offset animates.

## What keeps it deterministic

- There is one `gsap.timeline({ paused: true })`, registered at `window.__timelines["<root data-composition-id>"]` after it is built.
- `fromTo` puts the initial state in the tween, not in CSS. The value at any seek time comes only from the timeline position.
- The tween has an explicit position (`0`) and a finite duration. It uses no `Date.now()`, unseeded `Math.random()`, or `repeat: -1` without a finite root `data-duration`.
- If the heading is itself a `.clip`, animate an inner child rather than the clip (that rule concerns `autoAlpha`/`visibility`, but tweening a child is the safe pattern). Don't add a `visibility:hidden` exit set, because the runtime hides timed clips itself.

Fix A is the cleaner choice. It removes the conflict entirely rather than relying on GSAP to own the centering offset.

