GSAP writes `x`/`y` into the element's inline `transform`, which **replaces** the CSS `transform: translate(-50%, -50%)`. When the tween starts, the heading loses its centering offset and jumps down and to the right by half its own size. `lint` flags this pattern as `gsap_css_transform_conflict`.

## Fix A (preferred): center with layout, animate with transforms

Let a flex wrapper handle centering, so the heading's `transform` belongs only to GSAP.

```html
<div class="clip title-layer" data-start="0" data-duration="4" data-track-index="1">
  <h1 id="title-heading" class="heading">Your Title</h1>
</div>
```

```css
.title-layer {
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
.heading {
  display: block;   /* transformed elements must be block-level and sized */
  margin: 0;
  /* no transform here */
}
```

```js
const tl = gsap.timeline({ paused: true });
tl.fromTo("#title-heading",
  { x: -40, y: 20, opacity: 0 },
  { x: 0, y: 0, opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power3.out" }, 0);

window.__timelines["main"] = tl; // must match the root data-composition-id
```

## Fix B: keep absolute positioning, move the offset into GSAP

If the heading has to stay `position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%`, remove the CSS transform and let GSAP own both offsets. GSAP stacks `xPercent`/`yPercent` with `x`/`y`:

```css
.heading { position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; margin: 0; /* no transform */ }
```

```js
tl.fromTo("#title-heading",
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, x: -40, y: 20, opacity: 0 },
  { xPercent: -50, yPercent: -50, x: 0,   y: 0,  opacity: 1, duration: 0.8, ease: "power3.out" }, 0);
```

## Why both fixes are deterministic

- `fromTo` sets explicit start and end states, so seeking to any frame gives the same result and nothing depends on CSS values read at runtime.
- There is one paused timeline, registered at `window.__timelines["<root id>"]` once it has been built.
- Nothing uses clocks, unseeded randomness, or input state.
- The tween targets the heading, not the `.clip` wrapper, and no `autoAlpha`/`visibility` is tweened on a clip. This avoids `gsap_animates_clip_element`.

Afterwards, `npx hyperframes check` should no longer report `gsap_css_transform_conflict`.

